instructions:
You are working in the JavaScript library repo checked out at /luxon (Luxon date library, tests use jest, sources in /luxon/src, tests in /luxon/test). Node modules are already installed. Do NOT use the internet. Do NOT edit any file under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be modified). Only edit /luxon/src/interval.js.

TASK (two parts, do them in order):

PART 1 - BASELINE: run the existing test suite and record the result. Command: cd /luxon && npx jest 2>&1 | tail -40 . Record exactly which tests (if any) fail before any edit. Save that list in your report.

PART 2 - FIX THE BUG: `Interval.fromISO` throws when `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true` even for inputs that are valid intervals made of a date plus a duration. Repro:
  Settings.throwOnInvalid = true;
  Interval.fromISO("2020-06-22T17:30:00.000+02:00/PT5H30M");
  // expected: a valid Interval (same as with throwOnInvalid=false)
  // actual: throws InvalidDateTimeError: Invalid DateTime
Same for the reversed form "PT5H30M/2020-06-22T17:30:00.000+02:00".
Cause: in src/interval.js `static fromISO(text, opts)`, it calls `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` / `DateTime.fromISO(e, opts)` unconditionally; when one half is a duration string, DateTime.fromISO returns an invalid DateTime, and with throwOnInvalid=true `DateTime.invalid()` throws instead of returning an invalid instance. The code must tolerate that half failing to parse as a DateTime and fall back to parsing it as a Duration.

Apply this exact change to the `static fromISO(text, opts)` method body in /luxon/src/interval.js (keep the JSDoc comment above it untouched; keep the same 2-space/prettier style):

  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed as ISO 8601`);
  }

Note: the inner `catch (e)` shadows the outer `e` const deliberately (matching upstream style) - that is fine, but make sure the outer `const [s, e]` destructuring is NOT renamed, because the rest of the method uses `e` for the end half of the string.

VERIFY (all must pass):
1. cd /luxon && npx jest 2>&1 | tail -40  -> the set of failing tests must be exactly the same as the baseline (ideally zero failures).
2. Write a temporary scratch script OUTSIDE the test directory, e.g. /tmp/check.js, and run it with `cd /luxon && npx babel-node /tmp/check.js` (if babel-node is unavailable use `npx jest` with a scratch test placed at /tmp and --rootDir, or simply run node with an inline require of the built src via @babel/register; pick whatever works). It must check, with Settings.throwOnInvalid = true:
   - Interval.fromISO("2020-06-22T17:30:00.000+02:00/PT5H30M") is valid, its start is 2020-06-22T17:30:00.000+02:00 and its end is 2020-06-22T23:00:00.000+02:00 (i.e. start + 5h30m), and that it equals the result obtained with throwOnInvalid left false.
   - Interval.fromISO("PT5H30M/2020-06-22T17:30:00.000+02:00") is valid and its start is 2020-06-22T12:00:00.000+02:00 and end 2020-06-22T17:30:00.000+02:00.
   - Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/2016-12-31", { zone: "Europe/Paris" }) still works and start.zoneName === "Europe/Paris".
   - Interval.fromISO("2007-03-01T13:00:00/P1Y2M10DT2H30M") gives end 2008-05-11T15:30:00.
   - With throwOnInvalid = true, genuinely unparsable input such as "foo/bar" still throws (an InvalidIntervalError) - that is expected/documented behavior, not a bug.
   - With throwOnInvalid = false, "foo/bar" still returns an invalid Interval with invalidReason "unparsable".
3. Clean up any scratch files you created inside /luxon (leave /tmp files, they are outside the repo). Run `cd /luxon && git status --porcelain` and confirm the ONLY modified file is src/interval.js.

REPORT: the baseline failing-test list, the exact diff you applied (git diff), the full output summary of the final `npx jest` run (number of suites/tests passed/failed), the results of each verification check above, and `git status --porcelain` output. If anything fails, say precisely what and why.

